Right now you’re seeing the MVP version of this calculator.

I have a toggle panel where I can switch to the Final version — and just like that, we see the updated version without a full page reload or new deployment.

I can also configure it so that only 10% of users, or only beta users, see the Final version — great for testing or gradual rollout.

The flags persist using localStorage, so refreshing the page or navigating doesn’t reset the state.

This approach works perfectly with our current architecture: we use Contentful for content, Next.js for building the bundle, and WEM to host the static output.

Since this flag system is entirely browser-based, it doesn’t need a backend or server-side rendering — we can keep our static workflow and still ship feature-gated components.

And if something goes wrong, we don’t need to revert code — just flip the flag and we’re back to the MVP instantly.

This solves the interview’s original ask: how do we migrate complex UI faster and better?

Instead of replacing a form or calculator in one risky move, we deliver both versions and control release through flags.

It gives teams more autonomy, improves release safety, and makes testing in production far more controlled.

